.Dd September 5, 2026
.Dt PG_LASWELL_MCP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pg_laswell_mcp
.Nd lock-aware PostgreSQL migration executor, speaking MCP over stdio
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Ar conninfo
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
applies signed migration specifications to a PostgreSQL database.
It is not a generic migration runner: a specification states a business-level
.Em change intent ,
and
.Nm
reads the live catalog to decide how to apply it, based on what the target
table actually is rather than on what the statement says.
.Pp
The two properties that motivate it:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Em The safe method is a property of the table, not the statement .
.Ic CREATE INDEX
is instant on an empty table and an outage at a billion rows.
A script written elsewhere, against a database of a different size, cannot
know which it faces.
.It
.Em Pacing depends on contention, not on row count .
A backfill batched at a fixed number of rows per commit holds locks for
however long that many rows happens to take today.
It should instead commit when something starts waiting on it.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
is the executing half of a pair.
Its sibling,
.Xr pg_licht_mcp 1 ,
is read-only by invariant and can only advise; this program is allowed to act.
Use both: read load, locks and capacity through pg_licht, and apply change
through
.Nm .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c , Fl -config Ar file
Connection registry, in INI format.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
Configuration is resolved in this order, first match winning:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
.Fl -config Ar file
.It
.Ev PGLASWELL_CONFIG
.It
.Pa ~/.config/pg_laswell/laswell.ini ,
if it exists
.It
.Ev DATABASE_URL
.It
the first non-option argument
.El
.Sh POOLERS
The executor requires a
.Em direct
connection.
This is a departure from
.Xr pg_licht_mcp 1 ,
which is deliberately safe behind a transaction-mode pooler, and the reason
is not stylistic.
.Pp
Measured against PgBouncer on PostgreSQL 18.6: with
.Va pool_mode = transaction ,
a session GUC set by one client was readable by a
.Em different
client, and an advisory lock taken by one client was visible as held to
another.
PgBouncer does not run
.Va server_reset_query
in transaction mode unless
.Va server_reset_query_always
is enabled, and it is off by default.
Most operators do not know this.
.Pp
Job coordination in
.Nm
rests on session advisory locks, and
.Fn pg_try_advisory_lock
is re-entrant within a single session.
Two jobs sharing one server connection would therefore both believe they hold
the exclusive lock on the same table, with no error reported anywhere.
.Pp
Detection at startup is not achievable: comparing
.Fn pg_backend_pid
across two transactions has false negatives at both ends of the pool-size
range.
Instead the worker re-reads its backend PID at the start of every transaction
and aborts the job on any change.
A startup probe additionally looks for leaked session state across two
connections; a positive result is conclusive and refuses, but a negative
result proves nothing and is never reported as a direct connection having
been confirmed.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev DATABASE_URL
libpq connection string, used when no registry file is given.
.It Ev PGLASWELL_CONFIG
Path to the connection registry.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr psql 1
.Sh STANDARDS
Model Context Protocol, revisions 2024-11-05 through 2025-11-25, over stdio
using JSON-RPC 2.0.
